00:06:35.439 --> 00:06:38.010
is Project Jupyter and a
little bit about its history?

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:39.030
Where did it come from?

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.340
Because I know IPython
Notebooks originated

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.020
as one of the main
projects, but can you

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.490
tell us a little more
about all of this?

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:45.990
JESSICA FORDE: Well,
OK, I'll start.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:48.300
So back in, I think,
Colorado, there

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.740
was a physicist
named Fernando Perez,

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.590
who was working on his PhD.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:57.090
And he wanted to be able to
share his research with his

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.460
colleagues back
home in Medellín,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.510
Columbia and was frustrated by
the fact that the software he

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.192
was working with
was very expensive,

00:07:05.192 --> 00:07:07.650
and was closed source, and he
couldn't necessarily share it

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.880
very easily with
people back home.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.940
And so he, in his
spare time, created

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.940
IPython, which is a project
that still exists today

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.890
and is part of the
Jupyter ecosystem.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:21.780
And IPython is a command line
tool for interactive Python.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.080
It is now considered what
we call a kernel for people

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.240
to actually write code
in many other languages.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.630
In fact, you can even
create your own kernel.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:34.500
We just showed a blog
post with kernels in C++.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.040
So if you want to do interactive
C++, we have interactive C++.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.930
And from there, we got
more people involved.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:42.970
We have, as part
of our team also,

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:45.000
Brian Granger, who
also leads the project.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.860
And this eventually
became IPython Notebooks,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:52.470
which is a tool in the
browser for interactive Python

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.480
visualization data
science, which

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.980
now became renamed to Jupyter.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.890
Because again, we are actually
a polyglot open source project.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.980
And so we now, from
there, have a number

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.530
of projects related to
interactive computing,

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.650
open source, open
standards, and data science,

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.270
and scientific
communication, which

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.180
relate to education and
lots of other applications.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.130
And so some of our
latest projects,

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.234
Binder and JupyterHub, come out
of that sort of origin story.

00:08:17.234 --> 00:08:19.650
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: Yeah, the only
other thing I'd add to that

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.710
is that, as the technology
has grown in its complexity

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.950
and the variety of uses,
also the organization,

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.710
Jupyter itself, has
grown quite a bit.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.970
So as Jessica
mentioned, it originated

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:34.049
with a couple of
developers on extra side

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.390
projects kind of
time, but now is

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.870
a much more complex and
diversified group of people,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:43.169
some of which are researchers
at universities, some of which

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:46.200
are data scientists or
developers at companies,

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.122
and many of which are
just general open source

00:08:48.122 --> 00:08:50.580
enthusiasts who spend their
time working on these projects.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.220
So there are some core
members of the project,

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.220
but it has a much broader
open source community that

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.640
represents both data analytics
worlds, like Python, or R,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.160
or something like this, but
also a much broader spectrum

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:05.740
of languages, like
C++, or JavaScript,

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.440
or anything else
you could imagine.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.898
MARK MANDEL: Jessica,
you mentioned

00:09:08.898 --> 00:09:10.950
a couple of times the term
"interactive notebook"

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.570
as part of the Jupyter Project.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:14.610
What exactly does that mean?

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.440
JESSICA FORDE: So an
interactive notebook

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:21.900
is a browser interface in
which a person can enter code.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.640
In this example,
we'll take Python.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.850
And so let's say you are a
data scientist writing Python,

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:29.520
and you have your
Pandas dataframe,

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.350
and you want to be
able to visualize it.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.700
Now if you wanted to be able
to get the visualization on it,

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:37.200
you probably would have to do
a little bit of extra leg work

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.740
to get the image out, to save
it, to open it up and see it.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.810
Whereas, in an
interactive notebook,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.914
things flow together
relatively seamlessly so that,

00:09:45.914 --> 00:09:47.580
if you want to create
the plot, the plot

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:48.790
shows up in the browser.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.540
And so we really try to leverage
the power of the browser

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.762
to make scientific communication
a part of scientific computing.

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:58.720
MARK MANDEL: Is this
something that I would use

00:09:58.720 --> 00:09:59.910
standalone just myself?

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:02.284
Or is this something I would
do in partnership with, say,

00:10:02.284 --> 00:10:04.230
another developer kind
of Google Doc style?

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:05.356
Or how does that flow work?

00:10:05.356 --> 00:10:07.105
JESSICA FORDE: You
could do it either way.

00:10:07.105 --> 00:10:08.820
And in fact, people
do do it either way.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.459
Communication in a
I write something,

00:10:11.459 --> 00:10:13.000
and then I send it
to you or share it

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.330
to you is very, very popular.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:19.100
People also use notebooks
to write Scratch.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.350
Say you're trying
to prototype an idea

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.390
and you want to be able
to figure out if it runs

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.570
or if you like the way
the output looks to you,

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:28.136
you can do it that way.

00:10:28.136 --> 00:10:29.510
But additionally,
we actually are

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.450
very interested in ideas
of real-time collaboration,

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:33.040
as well.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.460
Today, that's
something that we're

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:37.790
working on and thinking about
in the Jupyter Lab project.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:40.490
Additionally, you can
communicate to people

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:44.780
within a certain ecosystem,
for example, within JupyterHub.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.851
So these kinds of relationships
between communicating

00:10:47.851 --> 00:10:49.850
for yourself and communicating
with other people

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:51.014
are very important to us.

00:10:51.014 --> 00:10:53.180
YUVI PANDA: So also, people
use it just with GitHub.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:54.920
So GitHub has
millions of notebooks.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:56.330
So people use it just like code.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.830
And GitHub even renders
them automatically now.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:00.510
So that's another way to use it.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.724
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: I think one
of the interesting origin

00:11:02.724 --> 00:11:04.640
stories of the Jupyter
Notebook actually comes

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.170
from scientific publishing.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:10.670
So one of the original ideas
that the then IPython, now

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.820
Jupyter team had was
trying to find a way

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:16.880
to package the
static representation

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.520
of your work, which in
science and academia

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:21.200
is just basically a PDF.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.910
It's a snapshot of
words that you write

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:24.830
and static images
that you generate.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.930
But behind those
static images is

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:28.610
a lot of really
interesting complexity.

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:30.830
And in some sense,
that's the real work.

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:34.880
Like, the code and the
operationalization of that code

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.950
is where the rubber meets
the road, so to speak.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.790
And so one of the goals of
the original notebook format

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:44.840
was to create an interface and
a way of packaging your work so

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:46.550
that you didn't
have to separate out

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:48.540
the code from the narrative.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.474
And that, hopefully, would
create different ways

00:11:51.474 --> 00:11:53.390
that you might try to
communicate your results

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:56.690
to other people in ways that
are only possible when you can

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:59.570
actively be interacting with
whatever work it is that you're

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:00.740
presenting to someone.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.531
JESSICA FORDE: And
we've been really lucky,

00:12:02.531 --> 00:12:03.980
in that we've had
very good uptake

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:07.820
from the scientific community,
from the academic community.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:09.920
I think they're in the
millions of Jupyter

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:11.340
Notebooks available on GitHub.

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.962
In fact, it's now considered
a language on GitHub.

00:12:13.962 --> 00:12:15.420
Although I don't
know necessarily--

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:15.919
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:15.919 --> 00:12:18.710
--if we think of it
as a language, per se,

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:22.310
because Jupyter Notebook can be
written in any kind of language

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:23.330
that we support.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:27.530
And we support tens of
languages, like over 100,

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:28.190
at least.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:31.520
And so we have notebooks
from the chief economist

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:33.770
of the World Bank
that are on GitHub.

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.980
We have notebooks from the
LIGO Project, which recently

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:41.670
won the physics Nobel Prize,
that are available on GitHub

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:43.099
that we also host on Binder.

00:12:43.099 --> 00:12:45.140
So there are a lot of
different interesting stuff

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:46.098
that's being happening.

00:12:46.098 --> 00:12:50.240
I know media companies now are
sharing their data journalism

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:51.740
through Jupyter Notebooks.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:54.020
And so we're really
excited about the ways

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:55.660
people have been
able to use it today.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:56.780
MELANIE WARRICK: Now you've
mentioned JupyterHub.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.670
Can you explain to us a little
bit more about what that is?

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:00.795
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: Yeah, sure.

00:13:00.795 --> 00:13:02.466
So a Jupyter
Notebook, for example,

00:13:02.466 --> 00:13:04.340
assumes that you're
running something locally

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:05.120
on your own computer.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:07.040
That's the most common
pattern of interaction

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.450
when you're writing code and
running it against some kernel.

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:12.470
What JupyterHub does
is it allows you

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.890
to post, either on hardware
that you own or somewhere

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:19.520
in the cloud, a server that
manages multiple Jupyter

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:20.850
processes at the same time.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:25.310
So it will use a Jupyter
server to have many users

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.650
simultaneously sending
code to kernels

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.110
and getting the
responses back from them.

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:34.220
It's useful partially because,
as a deployer of a JupyterHub,

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.770
you can specify the environment
that you want everyone

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:37.580
to have access to.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:40.160
You can specify the
packages, the versions.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:43.790
You can specify data that needs
to be in a particular place

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:45.870
so that everyone
has access to it.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:48.500
And the goal there is that,
by standardizing these things,

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:51.906
you're creating a more
consistent environment so

00:13:51.906 --> 00:13:53.780
that people can share
their work more easily,

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:55.655
and you don't run into
strange conflicts that

00:13:55.655 --> 00:13:57.770
come from having the
wrong package installed

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.050
or having the incorrect
version of data

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:01.394
on some path on your computer.

00:14:01.394 --> 00:14:03.810
And the other hope is that you
can use this, I should say,

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.070
as a portal to shared computing
infrastructure so that people

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:10.670
who don't have, say, fancy,
sophisticated hardware

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:12.680
themselves, or
maybe who don't even

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:16.520
have a laptop with a
ton of RAM, can still do

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:18.650
fairly complicated processing.

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.150
But they're doing
so via the cloud

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:23.840
or via whatever resources the
JupyterHub is deployed on.

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.150
And so I think the goal
there is that it increases

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:29.240
the accessibility towards
doing modern-day data

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.454
analytic interactive
workflows in a way

00:14:31.454 --> 00:14:32.870
that's only possible
when you have

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:34.280
those kind of shared resources.

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:35.420
MELANIE WARRICK: And when
we were talking earlier,

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:36.836
you were saying
how JupyterHub was

00:14:36.836 --> 00:14:39.560
sort of the impetus for
why you started to engage

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:41.660
with using Kubernetes.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:45.740
YUVI PANDA: So when I
started working at Berkeley,

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:47.169
we had a biggish course.

00:14:47.169 --> 00:14:47.960
We had like-- what?

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.510
900 students at that time?

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:51.390
They were learning
fundamental data science.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.360
And we wanted them
to use a JupyterHub,

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.210
because we didn't want to
spend our time teaching

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:57.380
people how to install stuff.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.660
And they might have
different kinds of computers.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:02.180
And then someone's going to
come up with the Windows XP,

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.850
and we had to figure out how
to install Python through that.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.220
So we wanted to eliminate
that, and then allows

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:10.375
students to focus directly
on learning data science.

00:15:10.375 --> 00:15:11.750
This is also
especially important

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.085
because we were
targeting groups that

00:15:13.085 --> 00:15:14.501
were not just in
computer science,

00:15:14.501 --> 00:15:16.550
but people from other
disciplines and whatnot.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:18.591
And it was like we were
running into some scaling

00:15:18.591 --> 00:15:20.040
problems at that time.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:23.290
And we also wanted to be as
cloud-agnostic as possible.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:26.310
We did not want to get locked
into any specific cloud vendor.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.890
And we also wanted to be able
to [INAUDIBLE] if we needed to.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.473
And so at this point, Kubernetes
was a very good choice for us

00:15:31.473 --> 00:15:34.120
to make because it let us do
all of this in one system.

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.930
So we didn't need to have like
Ansible or something else that

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:38.970
set up the base
system, and then have

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:42.060
like a clustering
technology on top which was

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:43.722
a lot more complex to manage.

00:15:43.722 --> 00:15:45.930
This was just like, OK, we
have a Kubernetes cluster,

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.859
we can do everything
we want on top of it.

00:15:47.859 --> 00:15:49.650
And the scalability is
[? fatly ?] elastic.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.725
You can go up and down
without too many problems.

00:15:51.725 --> 00:15:54.030
And so we were like,
OK, let's just--

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.160
I was already writing
the Cumulus integration

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:58.440
for JupyterHub for a
while as a volunteer.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:01.080
And then I just got hired at
Berkeley to do that full time.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:02.705
MARK MANDEL: So does
that mean that you

00:16:02.705 --> 00:16:04.936
have to have a Kubernetes
cluster to run JupyterHub?

00:16:04.936 --> 00:16:06.060
Or is it an optional thing?

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:07.226
YUVI PANDA: Not necessarily.

00:16:07.226 --> 00:16:08.930
So JupyterHub is
very extensible.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.040
You can plug in any
authenticator you want.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.980
And then, the same way, you can
plug in any spawner you want.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.510
So it ships with
a default spawner

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:16.980
that just doesn't
assume anything,

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.420
except you have
the next machine.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:22.290
But then there are spawners
for lots of popular technology.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.750
The spawners just use a
docker or a container.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:26.940
There's the Kubernetes
Spawner that we have written.

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:29.530
And then there's a
system despawner.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:30.760
There's lots of spawners.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:31.510
People write them.

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:32.610
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: I think
one of the challenges

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:34.524
that Data 8 faced
in particular is

00:16:34.524 --> 00:16:36.690
that, when you're designing
technology for something

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:40.380
like science or education,
you have to make assumptions

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.210
that the organizations
that are going

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.730
to be using that
technology don't have,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.649
oftentimes, as much resources
and funds to hire people

00:16:47.649 --> 00:16:50.190
as you would if you were at a
company or something like this.

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:52.170
And I think one of the
benefits of Kubernetes

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.990
is that, because of the
properties that people often

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:56.670
talk about in terms
of self-healing,

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.560
and scalability,
and stuff like this,

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:02.730
you can often manage more
complex deployments with fewer

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:06.329
highly-trained, technical,
DevOps-style people.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:10.140
And Data 8, as Yuvi mentioned,
now it's over, I think,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.470
1,400 students or
something like this.

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:15.990
And it has, by some accounts,
a relatively modest team

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:19.050
of people who are actively
developing it, and upgrading

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:20.730
it, and maintaining
it over time.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.740
And my intuition is
that that's something

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:25.290
that would be much
more difficult to do

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:29.504
if you were using other kinds
of cloud deployment approaches.

00:17:29.504 --> 00:17:31.170
MELANIE WARRICK: So
I know when we first

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.360
were talking about doing
this podcast, Jessica,

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:35.010
you were telling
me about Binder.

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:37.050
And I know Binder is
built off of JupyterHub.

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:39.716
So can you tell us a little more
about Binder, and what it does,

00:17:39.716 --> 00:17:40.720
and why it was built?

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:43.470
JESSICA FORDE: Yeah, so I'll
start with the story of Binder.

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:47.700
So Binder originally
started as a project

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.980
that was out of Janelia Farm.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:55.800
And it was a project to share
notebooks in a curated manner

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.660
so that you had
notebooks that had

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:00.330
a similar idea or a
similar narrative story

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:03.240
to come together in
one cohesive piece.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.960
And so the current
set-up we have now is

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:11.820
the version 2.0 of Binder, which
is a publicly-available service

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:16.170
for GitHub repos to share
with the public in a way that

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.600
has the entire environment
set up so that you would go

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:22.860
to a specific URL, and you are
launched in a docker container,

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:24.840
and that ends up giving
you the opportunity

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.480
to work with the
repo with everything

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:28.560
already pre-installed.

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.650
It's a similar experience to the
things that the Data 8 students

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:32.490
are getting.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:35.220
But in this case, you are
getting specifically the repo

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:37.620
that you are looking
at it from GitHub.

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:40.740
And this is particularly
interesting for applications

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:43.740
in science, and
education, also people

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.720
who are creating new libraries
and want to show it off.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.380
In fact, that's what we
used to show off JupyterLab.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:51.240
We ended up using
Binder and said,

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:53.280
if you want to try
out JupyterLab, here.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.180
We have it right now.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:55.895
You just click on
the URL, and you

00:18:55.895 --> 00:18:59.130
are taken to the user
interface of JupyterLab.

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:00.930
You don't need to
install anything.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:01.860
Here it is.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:02.580
Play with it.

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:03.670
See how you like it.

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:05.760
So that's particularly
interesting for us,

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:10.050
because it's a different way for
us to interact with the public,

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:13.050
providing open source
as a service for us

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:17.820
to share scientific data science
repositories through GitHub.

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:20.280
And in fact, Binder is
actually a number of projects

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.230
that are connected together.

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:23.490
There are modular pieces.

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:25.410
JupyterHub is
actually one of them.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:29.100
And the other major pieces
under it are repo to Docker,

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.940
the binder server itself.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:34.410
And then we also have front
ends such as Jupyter Notebooks,

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:36.325
RStudio and JupyterLab.

00:19:36.325 --> 00:19:37.950
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: It's
worth highlighting

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:41.820
that Binder was originally
created by a researcher named

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:42.810
Jeremy Freeman.

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:45.270
And it was also open
source technology.

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:47.580
It was running
online in the cloud.

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.200
And it performed
what Jessica just

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.930
described-- being able to
share an interactive data

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.480
analytic environment
with a single link.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:57.780
And because that
project was open source,

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:01.500
the Jupyter team started having
conversations with the first

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:03.360
incarnation of the
Binder project,

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:06.480
basically realizing that
a lot of the complexity

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:09.750
of the tooling in Binder
1.0-- we'll call it--

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:12.840
was doing a lot of this
stuff that JupyterHub

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:15.490
handles very flexibly
and very nicely already.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:18.400
And so there was kind of a
realization that, well, what

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:21.610
if we could just make Binder
a particular configuration

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:24.490
of a deployment of
a JupyterHub and add

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:27.550
one extra component, which is
the ability to automatically

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:30.400
generate your computing
environment on-the-fly so

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.540
that people can specify
their environment just

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:35.800
via random text files in
their GitHub repository,

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:38.500
rather than requiring
people to craft a Docker

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.160
image on their own?

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.500
So those projects
kind of fused together

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:46.450
in a collaboration that's
been going on for about a year

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:47.770
or so now.

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:49.870
But a lot of that initial
heavy lifting we're

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:51.954
all very thankful to the
original Binder team for.

00:20:51.954 --> 00:20:53.328
MELANIE WARRICK:
And I know, when

00:20:53.328 --> 00:20:55.660
we were talking about
Binder, the reason

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:58.480
behind driving out, building
up Binder 2-- or part

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:01.182
of the reason-- was you've
got so much great content

00:21:01.182 --> 00:21:03.640
and research that's being done
in the scientific community,

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:05.764
and there was a desire to
be able to make it easier

00:21:05.764 --> 00:21:07.450
for people to run
that kind of content

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:10.690
and run that without having
to recreate it from scratch.

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:12.190
JESSICA FORDE: Yeah,
I mean, I think

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:16.510
that one of the points of
friction in scientific research

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:19.750
is being able to
reproduce or being

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:23.890
able to work with the scientific
computation pipeline that

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:26.260
is in a particular paper.

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:29.380
So for example, the
LIGO Project is probably

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:31.639
our most famous
research institute

00:21:31.639 --> 00:21:33.430
who has their research
available on Binder.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:35.440
They have a demo
that allows people

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.620
to get immediate access
to their data, which

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:43.120
is gravitational wave data,
and show people basic signal

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:47.350
processing methods so that
you can reproduce their study

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:50.320
and basically find the
gravitational waves that they

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:51.970
got the Nobel Prize for.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.880
If people did not necessarily
have access to Binder,

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:56.520
it would be less interactive.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:59.830
One would have to figure out how
the dependencies were set up,

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:03.292
install everything from scratch,
and try to piece together

00:22:03.292 --> 00:22:04.750
all the different
parts of the repo

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:07.650
to understand how to reproduce
this result. Whereas,

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.570
with Binder and also
Jupyter Notebooks,

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:13.690
it becomes a seamless,
interactive notebook that

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:15.490
has everything pre-installed.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.070
And so you can walk yourself
through the thinking

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:20.890
of these scientists
and understand how they

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:22.210
conduct their own research.

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:22.540
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: Yeah.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:24.164
The way that I like
to think about this

00:22:24.164 --> 00:22:26.740
is there is, in my mind, a
difference between making

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:29.140
your work technically
open and reproducible,

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:32.050
versus making it practically
open and reproducible.

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:34.150
And a lot of times, you'll
see people just throw

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.760
a bunch of code and
maybe some description

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:38.510
of the environment
needed to run that code

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:40.360
onto the web somewhere.

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:41.890
And while it is
technically possible

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:44.170
that you could go through all
of the different steps of trying

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.670
to figure out what it is that
was going through the author's

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:48.700
head when they put
all of this there,

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:50.530
the vast majority
of people aren't

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:53.860
going to take the time
to clone a repository,

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.620
pull it onto their computer,
install all the packages that

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:59.054
are needed, get the data
into the right place,

00:22:59.054 --> 00:23:01.220
spin up their own session,
start sifting through it,

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:02.950
debug problems
when they come up.

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:04.670
Most people just won't do that.

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:07.360
But if you could do
something like share

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:09.850
the ability to interact
with that repository just

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:12.520
via a single link that someone
clicks and they can immediately

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:15.430
start getting up and
running, I think the barrier

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:18.220
to entry there is low enough
that people will actually

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:19.180
start doing it.

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.772
And then you can do
different kinds of things

00:23:21.772 --> 00:23:23.230
in terms of designing
your content,

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:24.490
when you're assuming
that others are going

00:23:24.490 --> 00:23:25.840
to be interacting with it.

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:28.660
JESSICA FORDE: And we
recently presented some work

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:31.420
at the Fairness,
Accountability and Transparency

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.820
Conference in New
York with regards

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:36.017
to accountability and
transparency in machine

00:23:36.017 --> 00:23:37.600
learning research,
although we believe

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:41.440
this extends to all different
kinds of scientific research

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.450
and data science in general.

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:45.690
But the interesting
distinction, I think,

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.002
in when we think
about what Binder does

00:23:48.002 --> 00:23:49.960
is that, historically
when people were thinking

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:52.210
about reproducibility,
they wanted

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:56.140
to be able to
independently validate

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.990
what happened in
a scientific study

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:01.790
doing it in their own laboratory
or their own environment.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:05.200
We're basically trying to
replicate the environment

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:08.380
of the scientist,
as much as possible

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.660
with modern
computational methods,

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:13.600
so it is as if the
laboratory is opening

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:15.970
the doors to the public, and
allowing people to walk in,

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:17.320
and says, these are our tools.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:18.730
This is what we did.

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:21.850
And you can use these tools
as if they were your own.

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:23.652
So it's kind of an
interesting distinction.

00:24:23.652 --> 00:24:25.360
And I think that's
part of the reason why

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:28.100
we think about it even as an
accountability and transparency

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:28.600
project.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.180
Because the
distinction is actually

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.197
that you're not
necessarily reproducing it

00:24:33.197 --> 00:24:34.780
in that, if it's not
a separate thing,

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:38.200
it is the exact same environment
as the scientist that

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:39.545
is producing this research.

00:24:39.545 --> 00:24:40.420
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:41.461
This sounds really great.

00:24:41.461 --> 00:24:43.870
So if I'm sitting down and
I'm doing some research,

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:46.080
and I have this data set
that I'm doing research on,

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:49.300
and I'm thinking to myself, I
want to get this out on Binder,

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:50.500
where do I put my data?

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:52.670
What does that development
pipeline look like?

00:24:52.670 --> 00:24:54.445
How do I get that
sort of thing set up?

00:24:54.445 --> 00:24:56.320
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: So one
of the goals of Binder

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:59.380
is to try and piggyback
on pre-existing tools

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:01.910
in the open source ecosystem
as much as possible.

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:05.380
And what that means is that
the delta, the amount of energy

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:08.170
that's needed to make a
repository, quote unquote,

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:10.210
"Binder ready" is pretty small.

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:13.150
What Binder does is, when
you give it a URL to a Git

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:16.292
repository-- and that could be
something on GitHub, or GitLab,

00:25:16.292 --> 00:25:18.250
or wherever you put your
code-- as long as it's

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:22.030
publicly available, it's going
to check out the repository.

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:24.480
And it looks for what are
called configuration files.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.590
And by that, what
I mean is if you're

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:28.930
a Python developer,
requirement.txt or

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:31.615
environment.yml, if
you're Julia developer,

00:25:31.615 --> 00:25:34.480
a capital REQUIRE file.

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:37.480
It also looks for things
like apt.txt files

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:40.150
to specify apt packages.

00:25:40.150 --> 00:25:43.150
And the goal of this is to
infer the environment that's

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.370
needed from text
files that are already

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:49.390
part of the workflows for people
from those various communities.

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:51.610
And so, from the
author's perspective,

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.710
if you want to make your
repository interactive

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:56.170
via something like
Binder, all you need to do

00:25:56.170 --> 00:25:58.240
is make sure that those
text files are there

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:00.400
in either the root
of your repository

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:03.242
or in a folder in the root
of your repo called Binder.

00:26:03.242 --> 00:26:05.200
Binder is then just going
to automatically look

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:06.033
through those files.

00:26:06.033 --> 00:26:09.250
And whenever it finds them, it
generates a Docker file that

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:11.870
says, OK, I need this
version of NumPy installed

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:13.840
and this version of
Matplotlib installed.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.690
It then generates a Docker
image from that Docker file

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:17.890
and registers it online.

00:26:17.890 --> 00:26:20.380
And then Binder knows how to
ask for certain images based

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:23.140
on the links that different
users are clicking on.

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:26.150
So in many cases, you don't
actually have to do anything.

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:28.360
If you've already
followed best practices

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:30.310
in scientific computing,
you've already

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:34.330
included an environment.yml
file with your repository.

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:35.740
In many cases,
all you need to do

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:39.084
is just give that URL to
Binder, and it'll do the rest.

00:26:39.084 --> 00:26:40.750
MARK MANDEL: And where
do I put my data?

00:26:40.750 --> 00:26:42.460
Especially if I have
very large sets of data,

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:43.251
where does that go?

00:26:43.251 --> 00:26:45.070
Does it go on my
GitHub repository?

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:46.660
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: I think that if
you asked 10 different people,

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:48.677
you would get 10 different
responses to that.

00:26:48.677 --> 00:26:49.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:52.150
JESSICA FORDE: The examples
that we've seen so far

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:54.760
generally use
smaller sets of data,

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:57.580
just because the amount of
computational power we have

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.080
is rather limited.

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:01.150
So the work that
we've done so far

00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:06.220
on showing how people can use
Binder has been on projects

00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:09.160
where the size of the
data is relatively limited

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:12.476
and the amount of computation
that a person is finally using

00:27:12.476 --> 00:27:13.600
at the tail end is limited.

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.660
So we won't necessarily
be able to reproduce

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:20.590
on Binder a study that
uses an entire server

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:22.060
farm, for example.

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:26.080
But if you do have these pieces
and parts, the computation

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:29.050
then, since it is
interactive, can be modified.

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:32.140
So for example, in the write-up
we had for the Fairness,

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:33.760
Accountability and
Transparency study,

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:38.950
we took a [? HKai ?] paper
that had relatively simple

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:40.480
experiments computationally.

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:41.500
They weren't expensive.

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:45.220
And we said, OK, let's try
modifying this experiment

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:47.350
and seeing how this
works, which is actually

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:51.160
very interesting because, in a
lot of studies, you are given,

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:52.390
this is what we did.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:52.990
It's done.

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:53.660
There it is.

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:56.320
But the way Binder
works, because it

00:27:56.320 --> 00:27:59.080
uses interactive computing and
it has the same environment,

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:03.190
it says, now that you have this
tool, this model, this idea,

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:06.280
we give you the
ability to interact

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:09.700
with the research in a way
that people haven't been

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:14.980
able to do online in a
publication-like experience,

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:17.020
which is particularly
interesting, I think,

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:22.750
because it allows people to
be able to think beyond what

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:24.970
the researchers are
simply telling you

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:28.956
and work with the repo in
a way that you might want

00:28:28.956 --> 00:28:30.580
to be able to work
with it on your own,

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:32.740
that you necessarily
weren't able to do it

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:35.837
because it required so much
leg work to get to that point.

00:28:35.837 --> 00:28:37.420
MELANIE WARRICK: But
is it built to be

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:38.710
able to work with server farms?

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:39.880
I mean, is it built
to be able to hook

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:40.630
into that kind of thing?

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:41.338
YUVI PANDA: Yeah.

00:28:41.338 --> 00:28:44.830
It ultimately uses JupyterHub
to actually run your code.

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:47.016
And JupyterHub can hook
into anything you want.

00:28:47.016 --> 00:28:48.390
At least right
now, because we've

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:49.870
done it as a free
public service,

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:52.960
we put limits on how much RAM
you can use and all of that.

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:55.870
But you can set up one for your
own institution or whatever,

00:28:55.870 --> 00:28:57.400
and then give it
as much resources

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:00.370
as you want running on
whatever kind of infrastructure

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:01.570
you need.

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:04.690
So you could, for example,
just configure your JupyterHub

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:05.860
to use--

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:08.420
I think there is a
Google Cloud VM spawner.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:10.935
So then everyone basically
gets one entire machine.

00:29:10.935 --> 00:29:13.060
Or you can just configure
it to, like, OK, everyone

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:15.787
gets an instance group that can
scale up and down as they want.

00:29:15.787 --> 00:29:16.620
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: Yes.

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.578
JESSICA FORDE: And then
to your question, Mark,

00:29:18.578 --> 00:29:20.800
you can integrate with any
kind of data repositories

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:21.852
that exist out there.

00:29:21.852 --> 00:29:22.560
YUVI PANDA: Yeah.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:22.860
Yeah.

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:24.818
It's just a matter of
what you do in your code.

00:29:24.818 --> 00:29:27.200
I know, for example, there
is the Pangea Project there.

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:28.960
And that's a funded
project that tries

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:32.570
to make a central platform for
people who do Earth sciences.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:37.040
And so they store all their data
in GCS, Google Cloud Storage.

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:41.380
And so they have fuse drivers
that let them get data directly

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:42.480
from GCS.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.870
And the UK Met Office
has something similar.

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:47.860
But they're an S3, so they
get their data out of S3.

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:49.750
And this is all
agnostic to JupyterHub.

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:52.000
We don't actually
care how we do this.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.340
JESSICA FORDE: Actually, also,
one of the earliest users

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:57.070
of JupyterHub for scientific
computing and research

00:29:57.070 --> 00:29:59.560
is actually the labs in
the Department of Energy.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:01.810
They end up doing a lot of
high-performance computing.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:03.550
And they have a
lot of scientists

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:06.520
who aren't DevOps people,
who aren't necessarily

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:10.630
the most sophisticated when it
comes to running complex jobs.

00:30:10.630 --> 00:30:13.510
And they want to be able
to have as lightweight

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:14.754
of an experience as possible.

00:30:14.754 --> 00:30:16.670
And JupyterHub was a
really great way for them

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:19.442
to have access to really
powerful Jupyter Notebooks.

00:30:19.442 --> 00:30:20.900
MELANIE WARRICK:
Is this only meant

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:22.640
for scientific communities?

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:24.720
Or have you seen
use cases outside?

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:26.345
YUVI PANDA: So, I
don't think it's only

00:30:26.345 --> 00:30:27.636
meant for scientific computing.

00:30:27.636 --> 00:30:29.120
So I came into this
from Wikimedia,

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:31.376
and so it's like a very,
oh, free knowledge.

00:30:31.376 --> 00:30:33.000
Everyone should be
able to participate.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.690
And so I was, at that time,
helping run this thing

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:37.760
called Two Labs,
which is we provide

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:39.890
free compute for people
who want to do things

00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:40.800
with Wikimedia data.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:43.700
So for example, a lot of the
anti-vandalism bots run here.

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:45.400
A lot of the statistics
are run here.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:48.200
But we required people do
[? SSI chain ?] and then

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:49.340
use Grid Engine.

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:50.570
And that was complex.

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:51.967
It was excluding
a lot of people.

00:30:51.967 --> 00:30:54.050
So from our perspective
at the time, we were like,

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:57.560
we wanted more people to
be able to access our data

00:30:57.560 --> 00:30:59.060
and do things with it.

00:30:59.060 --> 00:31:00.740
And so a good solution
was JupyterHub.

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:02.000
So that's where I
actually started work

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:04.730
on the Kubernetes stuff, because
we had a Kubernetes cluster.

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:06.980
And we're like, OK, let's
put JupyterHub on this

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:10.850
so we can securely provide
access to our data to people.

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:12.620
So we provided
access to our dumps

00:31:12.620 --> 00:31:16.730
but even to our direct MySQL
database, so which was live.

00:31:16.730 --> 00:31:18.500
We redacted all the
sensitive information

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:19.910
and gave people access to that.

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:22.244
And that's something you
cannot do on your own computer,

00:31:22.244 --> 00:31:24.576
because that's a data store
that's only available there.

00:31:24.576 --> 00:31:25.460
It's really large.

00:31:25.460 --> 00:31:26.829
It's updated in real time.

00:31:26.829 --> 00:31:29.120
And there's, I think, like
4 million edits or something

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:30.911
that people have made
from that JupyterHub.

00:31:30.911 --> 00:31:32.910
So that's, I think,
a very good use case.

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:34.670
And I think there's more
people doing things like that.

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:36.470
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: I think it's
worth highlighting again--

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:38.600
because I think this is actually
related to your previous point,

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:39.200
too--

00:31:39.200 --> 00:31:41.090
the goal of the
Jupyter ecosystem

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.250
is to build open source
building blocks that

00:31:43.250 --> 00:31:45.680
can be composed and
used for whatever use

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:46.880
case you might have in mind.

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:49.460
And so sometimes that is
doing scientific research.

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:51.560
Sometimes that is
teaching a class,

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:53.420
either at the
university level or even

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:56.340
at the elementary school
and middle school levels.

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:59.420
Sometimes it means providing
an interactive environment

00:31:59.420 --> 00:32:02.180
to connect with some
resources or hardware that

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:04.340
would otherwise be very
difficult to connect to,

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:07.340
like what Yuvi just described
with the Wikipedia data set.

00:32:07.340 --> 00:32:10.689
And related then to your
question about storage,

00:32:10.689 --> 00:32:12.230
our goal, the Jupyter
Project's goal,

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:15.770
is not to create completely
tightly integrated full stack

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:16.700
solutions.

00:32:16.700 --> 00:32:20.240
Our goal is to build pieces that
can be put together in order

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:21.480
to accomplish some goal.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:22.880
And so what I would say--

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:24.920
when people ask,
how is JupyterHub

00:32:24.920 --> 00:32:28.100
going to handle large-scale
storage, part of my answer

00:32:28.100 --> 00:32:29.691
is, well, we don't
really have to.

00:32:29.691 --> 00:32:31.190
We just need to
make sure that, when

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:33.170
there is another
open tool that exists

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.060
that makes that
possible, that it's

00:32:35.060 --> 00:32:38.787
easy to integrate that into
the JupyterHub Binder workflow.

00:32:38.787 --> 00:32:41.120
MELANIE WARRICK: Do you have
any features, functionality

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:44.220
that are up-and-coming, that
are in the future of JupyterHub,

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:46.422
Binder, Project
Jupyter, in general?

00:32:46.422 --> 00:32:47.630
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: We have lots.

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:48.480
MELANIE WARRICK: Anything
that you want to talk about?

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:48.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:49.945
MARK MANDEL: Lots is good.

00:32:49.945 --> 00:32:51.685
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:51.685 --> 00:32:53.810
YUVI PANDA: So one thing
that we've been working on

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:55.280
is high scale.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:58.340
So we're trying to get
up to 50,000 active users

00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:00.650
at a time, spanning
multiple Kubernetes

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:03.320
clusters and multiple
hubs, but all

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:06.020
[? pressing ?] the user as one.

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:07.360
So that's like one idea.

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:10.929
We put lot of work
into it at the moment.

00:33:10.929 --> 00:33:12.470
On the Binder side,
I think something

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:14.960
that we did fairly recently
and it's not super public

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:16.495
is we added RStudio support.

00:33:16.495 --> 00:33:18.620
Because I think a lot of
people in the R community,

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:21.470
they prefer using RStudio,
rather than Jupyter Notebooks.

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:23.030
And so we wanted to be able to--

00:33:23.030 --> 00:33:25.760
you know, we don't want to force
people to switch tools just

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:26.927
because they want to use us.

00:33:26.927 --> 00:33:29.051
So we were like, OK, let's
make this generic enough

00:33:29.051 --> 00:33:31.250
so that people can use
RStudio, or whatever else it

00:33:31.250 --> 00:33:32.780
is that they want to use.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:34.820
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: I think
that another interesting

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:37.070
future direction
for development--

00:33:37.070 --> 00:33:39.312
Yuvi just described
what would often be

00:33:39.312 --> 00:33:40.520
described as scaling outward.

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:42.936
So we're just trying to get
more, and more, and more users

00:33:42.936 --> 00:33:44.420
for a given
JupyterHub deployment.

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:47.630
But especially in more
sophisticated data science

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:50.510
teams or in academic
research scientific research,

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:53.900
you do need access to
non-trivial sized data sets.

00:33:53.900 --> 00:33:56.720
Or you need access to
high-performance clusters,

00:33:56.720 --> 00:33:57.986
and things like this.

00:33:57.986 --> 00:33:59.360
And so I think
that there's going

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:02.960
to be a push of development
towards connecting JupyterHub,

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:07.010
or something like Binder, with
more sophisticated hardware

00:34:07.010 --> 00:34:09.650
or more sophisticated
cloud infrastructure

00:34:09.650 --> 00:34:11.870
for doing computations
and analyzing data

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:12.949
on that hardware.

00:34:12.949 --> 00:34:15.409
And I'm excited to see the
different kinds of uses

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:18.170
that people come up with it,
as the ecosystem of tools that

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:20.239
are natively
running in the cloud

00:34:20.239 --> 00:34:21.790
continues to develop further.

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:24.415
YUVI PANDA: I also want to say,
one of our biggest new features

00:34:24.415 --> 00:34:25.397
is more documentation.

00:34:25.397 --> 00:34:25.980
I think it's--

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:26.510
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:34:26.510 --> 00:34:28.051
YUVI PANDA: --a lot
better documented

00:34:28.051 --> 00:34:30.290
now than it was six
months to a year ago.

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:32.460
Jessica, Chris, and
[INAUDIBLE],, who's not here,

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:35.239
have been doing a great
job of making sure

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:38.360
that the only information is
not in some chat somewhere

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:41.270
or hidden up in someone's
brain, but written out in ways

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:44.270
that a diverse group of people
can actually understand and be

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.100
used in their own contexts.

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:46.295
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:34:46.295 --> 00:34:48.253
What has it been like
working on an open source

00:34:48.253 --> 00:34:49.320
project for all of you?

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:51.999
And it sounds like
multiple, in some cases.

00:34:51.999 --> 00:34:54.290
YUVI PANDA: I've been working
on open source projects--

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:56.810
I was working on GNOME
when I was like 19.

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:58.230
And I was working at Wikimedia.

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:00.293
So I don't know what
it's like to not

00:35:00.293 --> 00:35:01.584
work on an open source project.

00:35:01.584 --> 00:35:02.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:03.710
So I'm kind of an outlier.

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:05.900
But Jupyter is the
first project that I'm

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:09.090
on that's partially
based in academia.

00:35:09.090 --> 00:35:11.160
So that's a little different.

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.710
And it's also a much
more smaller project.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:15.210
When I started
working at Wikimedia,

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:16.330
it was already very big.

00:35:16.330 --> 00:35:17.880
MELANIE WARRICK: But
how is it different?

00:35:17.880 --> 00:35:19.629
YUVI PANDA: I think,
at a smaller project,

00:35:19.629 --> 00:35:24.700
you have more responsibilities
to be kinder to everyone

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:26.250
than you have at
a larger project.

00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:28.500
Now, I'm not saying
that it's OK to be mean

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:29.970
to people on a larger project.

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:33.870
But I think if you are
person 40 in a project,

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:35.940
or person 60 in a
project, then you

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:37.680
have more responsibilities
than if you're

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:39.150
person 800 in a project.

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:41.790
If you're person 800, the
culture is already sort of set.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:43.710
Changing that is going
to be an uphill battle.

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:45.030
And it's going to be hard.

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:47.010
While if you're at a
smaller-person project,

00:35:47.010 --> 00:35:49.500
then it's much easier to set.

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:51.450
So if you are not
careful, then you

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:53.860
can set it in ways that
you don't want it to be.

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:56.112
So I think that's the
biggest difference.

00:35:56.112 --> 00:35:57.820
There's technical
differences, of course.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:00.420
When I was at Wikimedia,
I was the 16th person

00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:01.830
to join the ops team.

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:04.100
And I was the least
experienced person doing that.

00:36:04.100 --> 00:36:06.964
While here, I think, most
people don't have that much ops

00:36:06.964 --> 00:36:08.880
knowledge, because they
come through, like us,

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:11.050
like grad students and whatnot.

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:12.996
So that was also a
big difference for me.

00:36:12.996 --> 00:36:14.870
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: But I
think, related to that,

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:17.280
from my perspective, one
of the most exciting things

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:20.010
about the Jupyter Project--
and probably open source,

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:23.400
in general-- is that because of
the open nature of the project,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.410
you get a lot more
voices in the room.

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:26.970
And you get a lot
of representation

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:28.810
from different kinds of
background in the room.

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:30.226
So I think it's
amazing that I get

00:36:30.226 --> 00:36:33.000
to work on a team of
people, some of which

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:36.480
are heavily ops oriented and
have an incredible background

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:39.360
in Kubernetes, some of
which care about things

00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:42.090
like documentation, and
community growth, and the more

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:44.370
social aspects of open
source, some of whom

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:47.760
are more domain scientists
who run analytics in Python

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.600
or in R that wouldn't be
able to deploy a Kubernetes

00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:52.020
cluster by themselves.

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:55.530
Being able to coordinate that
kind of chaotic, diverse group

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:58.080
of people in a way that
you can create tools that

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:02.040
are truly community-driven
and also available

00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:05.070
for all kinds of use cases
is a really satisfying thing

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:07.230
and, in some ways
is, in my mind,

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:10.260
the primary goal of the
academic, and education,

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:12.737
and scientific communities--
creating public goods

00:37:12.737 --> 00:37:14.820
that people can then build
on for whatever purpose

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:15.780
they have in mind.

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:16.620
MELANIE WARRICK: How
do you coordinate?

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:18.240
Or how does that
get coordinated?

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:20.430
JESSICA FORDE: I think
that each sub-project

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:24.450
has their own subculture
and their own sub-methods.

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:27.570
And so that largely
determines how it works.

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:30.810
So actually, like, for example,
for me, I belong to, I think,

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:35.220
like five different Jupyter
Project organizations.

00:37:35.220 --> 00:37:38.400
A lot of people don't even know
that we have multiple repos.

00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:40.170
I think we have over 100.

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:42.700
We have at least five
or six organizations.

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:46.550
So each organization has
their own norms and culture,

00:37:46.550 --> 00:37:49.410
but we actually share
the same code of conduct.

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:53.640
So at the higher level,
values, norms, things like that

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:54.540
are shared.

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:55.830
And we have a governance repo.

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:57.960
If you want to spy
on how we work,

00:37:57.960 --> 00:37:59.560
it's all available
to the public.

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:01.810
In fact, all our meetings
are available to the public.

00:38:01.810 --> 00:38:03.480
We've put them
online on YouTube.

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:07.560
So our big, weekly meetings
are every Tuesday morning,

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:08.580
9:00 AM Pacific.

00:38:08.580 --> 00:38:10.512
You can watch us online.

00:38:10.512 --> 00:38:12.970
But each individual project,
then, has their own sub-norms.

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:14.880
So for example, there's
a monthly meeting

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:17.570
that we have with the
JupyterHub and Binder group.

00:38:17.570 --> 00:38:19.390
JupyterLab meets every week.

00:38:19.390 --> 00:38:22.200
And we have a one-hour
meeting once a week.

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:27.810
But some groups just mostly
work through the mailing list

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:29.390
and the issues.

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:32.340
And so it's all a combination
of all these things.

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:34.977
I guess one of the
things that we probably

00:38:34.977 --> 00:38:37.560
haven't mentioned enough, that
we probably should say more is,

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:39.490
again, we are an
open source project.

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:44.230
And so anybody can basically
jump in at any time.

00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:45.700
And so we really try to do that.

00:38:45.700 --> 00:38:47.241
And I think that's
also a lot of what

00:38:47.241 --> 00:38:51.810
I do is I basically try to
Tom Sawyer people into saying,

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:52.760
do you like Jupyter?

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:54.210
You can work for us, too.

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:56.820
And basically, I get them
to do my work for me.

00:38:56.820 --> 00:38:58.746
But really, it's
something that's

00:38:58.746 --> 00:38:59.870
very, very important to us.

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:01.650
In fact, we're having
a community day,

00:39:01.650 --> 00:39:04.080
I think, the 25th of
August in New York, which

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:06.570
is a free day for anyone
who wants to show up.

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:09.070
Or if you just want to
show up on the internet,

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:12.720
we will be actively
working on issues

00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:14.140
that's community related.

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:16.500
And we follow GitHub's
norms of marking issues

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:18.870
as first issue or help wanted.

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:20.890
So those things are
particularly standard.

00:39:20.890 --> 00:39:24.840
But again, the broader other
kinds of implementations

00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:26.549
are slightly different
from team to team.

00:39:26.549 --> 00:39:28.673
MELANIE WARRICK: And then,
were there any resources

00:39:28.673 --> 00:39:31.190
that you wanted to mention in
regards to JupyterHub, Jupyter

00:39:31.190 --> 00:39:31.770
Notebook?

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:34.050
We were talking about some of
the classes that are out there.

00:39:34.050 --> 00:39:34.800
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
where would people

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:37.090
go if they want to learn
about these projects?

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:39.298
CHRIS HOLDGRAF: So I think
that, in terms of the sort

00:39:39.298 --> 00:39:42.600
of examples of particular
deployments of JupyterHubs

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:46.920
for particular use cases,
if you go to data8.org--

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:49.680
just data, then the
number 8 dot org--

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:53.370
that is a public-facing
version of Data 8,

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:55.710
which is this major
course at UC Berkeley

00:39:55.710 --> 00:39:57.540
that Yuvi has been
working on and that

00:39:57.540 --> 00:39:59.730
has driven a lot of the
technical infrastructure,

00:39:59.730 --> 00:40:02.550
particularly on the
Kubernetes side of JupyterHub.

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:07.020
There's also mybinder.org
is the public service

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:08.070
version of Binder.

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:10.860
So as Jessica
mentioned, BinderHub

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:12.750
is open source
technology that anybody

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:14.790
could use for whatever
purpose they want

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:17.000
to to deploy a Binder server.

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:20.090
Mybinder.org is a kind of
technical demonstration

00:40:20.090 --> 00:40:22.712
and a free public
service where, as a user,

00:40:22.712 --> 00:40:24.170
you can begin to
interact with that

00:40:24.170 --> 00:40:26.570
and create shareable,
public links for your code

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:27.740
repositories.

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:30.110
And then, from a
developer's standpoint

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:32.570
or from a deployer's
standpoint, the best way

00:40:32.570 --> 00:40:35.060
to learn how to deploy
JupyterHub on Kubernetes

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:37.640
is at-- there's a guide
called "Zero to JupyterHub,"

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:42.244
which you can find
at z2jh.jupyter.org.

00:40:42.244 --> 00:40:45.170
And on the flip
side, for BinderHub,

00:40:45.170 --> 00:40:48.990
there is a guide to deploying
BinderHub on Kubernetes

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:51.297
at binderhub.readthedocs.io.

00:40:51.297 --> 00:40:52.880
JESSICA FORDE: Also,
I should probably

00:40:52.880 --> 00:40:55.550
mention another project
on the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:59.360
We have repo to Docker, and
that is also under the GitHub

00:40:59.360 --> 00:41:01.074
organization JupyterHub.

00:41:01.074 --> 00:41:02.990
A lot of the projects
we've been talking about

00:41:02.990 --> 00:41:05.690
have been under the GitHub
Organization JupyterHub.

00:41:05.690 --> 00:41:08.870
So if you go to
github.com/jupyterhub,

00:41:08.870 --> 00:41:10.370
you'll see a number
of our projects.

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:12.030
And repo to Docker
is one of them,

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:13.640
which is also
particularly useful

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:16.260
if you want to
take a GitHub repo

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:18.260
and turn it into a Docker
file or something that

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:21.349
can be easily used and shared
in that kind of format.

00:41:21.349 --> 00:41:22.640
MELANIE WARRICK: This is great.

