00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:56.928
MARK MANDEL: Yay!

00:42:56.928 --> 00:43:00.410
All right, how did
Google's predictions

00:43:00.410 --> 00:43:01.599
do during March Madness?

00:43:01.599 --> 00:43:03.140
So actually, let's
have some context.

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:06.350
I know March Madness, big
basketball-- there we go--

00:43:06.350 --> 00:43:07.390
basketball tournament.

00:43:07.390 --> 00:43:08.840
Google Cloud is a sponsor.

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:11.120
And there's a bunch of
machine learning stuff that

00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:12.974
was put in place
to see if we could

00:43:12.974 --> 00:43:15.265
predict who was going to win
and who was going to lose.

00:43:15.265 --> 00:43:15.540
Is that right?

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:16.100
MELANIE WARRICK: That's right.

00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:17.766
And there was also a
COWGILL competition

00:43:17.766 --> 00:43:19.880
that's been going
on to allow others

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:21.320
to run their own
prediction models

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:24.350
and see how effective they
were in March Madness.

00:43:24.350 --> 00:43:27.470
But Google did do its own
separate prediction model

00:43:27.470 --> 00:43:29.780
and worked hard at trying
to be able to come up

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:31.340
with real-time predictions, too.

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:34.160
Because during March
Madness, apparently,

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:37.130
during the halftime,
they would take the data

00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:39.470
from the first half and
feed it into the model

00:43:39.470 --> 00:43:42.180
and come back with predictions
prior to the second half.

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:42.680
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:44.305
MELANIE WARRICK:
During the Final Four,

00:43:44.305 --> 00:43:45.740
there was a couple
of halftime ads

00:43:45.740 --> 00:43:48.410
that were even generated
within the "Halftime Show"

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:51.320
to then show what they
thought the predictions were.

00:43:51.320 --> 00:43:53.600
And apparently, they
had a prediction

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:56.030
where Loyola versus
Michigan were playing.

00:43:56.030 --> 00:43:57.710
And they predicted 29 rebounds.

00:43:57.710 --> 00:43:59.570
And they were right.

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:02.290
And then they also predicted
during Villanova and Michigan

00:44:02.290 --> 00:44:05.660
that at least 21 three-pointer
attempts would be made.

00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:07.700
And the final count was 24.

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:09.520
So anyways, it was
real interesting.

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:09.790
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:11.456
MELANIE WARRICK: And
there's a blog post

00:44:11.456 --> 00:44:14.270
that helps give some insights
in terms of how the prediction

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:16.769
model was built. And this is
actually something you would do

00:44:16.769 --> 00:44:19.520
with, typically, any kind of
data science project in terms

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:22.970
of figure out what type of
data you need, then building up

00:44:22.970 --> 00:44:25.370
the pipeline that you're going
to need to feed the data,

00:44:25.370 --> 00:44:28.460
and build out the
model, and train it,

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:31.000
and then deploying
it into production.

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:33.140
And so they had this
model they trained off

00:44:33.140 --> 00:44:38.312
of 200,000 discrete files from a
decade of NCAA basketball data.

00:44:38.312 --> 00:44:39.020
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:40.310
MELANIE WARRICK: They
apparently engineered

00:44:40.310 --> 00:44:42.410
over 800 potential
features, and then applied

00:44:42.410 --> 00:44:45.020
variance and univariant
statistical tests.

00:44:45.020 --> 00:44:47.060
And they built out
their models using

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:51.110
these more standard regression
and classification techniques.

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:53.390
And when the games
were actually playing,

00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:55.980
they were updating
their entire game

00:44:55.980 --> 00:44:58.160
state every two
seconds and including

00:44:58.160 --> 00:44:59.600
all play-by-play data.

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:01.040
And they were
using Cloud Spanner

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:03.530
to help with this
kind of processing

00:45:03.530 --> 00:45:05.840
that they needed to do,
as well as BigQuery.

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:07.429
So like I said,
there's a blog post.

00:45:07.429 --> 00:45:09.470
We're going to include
that blog post so that you

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:12.980
can see how specifically
they actually engineered

00:45:12.980 --> 00:45:15.660
their prediction models,
how it was deployed.

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:17.670
And it'll give you
some great insights

00:45:17.670 --> 00:45:20.710
to think about when you're doing
your own type of data analysis,

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:22.110
data prediction modeling.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:23.340
And yeah, it's kind of cool.

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:23.790
MARK MANDEL: That is cool.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:23.890
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's kind of cool

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:24.720
what the results looked like.

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:26.219
It's kind of cool
how they built it.

00:45:26.219 --> 00:45:27.720
And congrats to the team.

